6. Repartition vs Coalesce
=> The difference between repartition() and coalesce() in Spark is a fundamental concept in performance optimization and data shuffling.
🟨 1. repartition()
✅ Description:
repartition(n) reshuffles the data across n partitions with a full shuffle, which means all data is re-distributed across the cluster. 
This transformation is wide because it involves shuffling across nodes.
🔧 Internal Behavior:
Breaks all current partitioning.
Randomly re-distributes the data.
Uses a round-robin or hash partitioning (if column-based).
Ensures even distribution of data across all n partitions.
df = df.repartition(10)  # full shuffle, evenly splits data into 10 partitions
df = df.repartition("country")  # repartition by column using hash partitioner

📉 Pros:
Good for increasing parallelism.
Helps in load balancing before wide transformations like groupBy, join.

⚠️ Cons:
Expensive due to full shuffle.
Can slow down jobs significantly if used unnecessarily.

🟩 2. coalesce()
✅ Description:
coalesce(n) reduces the number of partitions by merging adjacent ones. It avoids full shuffling, which makes it efficient, especially when decreasing partitions.

🔧 Internal Behavior:
Reuses existing partitions.
Merges them without data movement across nodes.
It’s a narrow transformation, meaning it doesn’t shuffle data.
df = df.coalesce(1)  # merge into 1 partition, e.g., before writing to a single file

📉 Pros:
Fast and efficient when reducing partition count.
Ideal for writing output to fewer files.

⚠️ Cons:
Doesn’t evenly distribute data.
May cause data skew if merged into too few partitions.





























































